{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-121840","model_call":26,"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_1166261270#0_2596816154"},"record":{"query":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_1166261270#0_2596816154","op":"document"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_53_1166261270#0_2596816154","document":"State assessments in 2021 — What you need to know – Team Duval News ... State assessments in 2021 — What you need to know State assessments in 2021 — What you need to know What are progress-monitoring tests? Why does the district want students to come to school for the last round of progress monitoring? Will COVID-19 prevention protocols be in place? Are these tests required? What are the consequences of opting out of the state exams? Do seniors need these exams to graduate? Will the state waive these requirements because of COVID-19? Can any of these tests be taken at home? If students opt out of the tests, are there repercussions for the school or the teachers? What should parents and guardians do?  ... State assessments in 2021 — What you need to know – Team Duval News State assessments in 2021 — What you need to know Tracy Pierce / February 9, 2021 / District Feb. 9, 2021 – The Florida statewide assessments season is approaching, and Duval County Public Schools seeks to work with its students and families to be ready. While the Florida Department of Education has allowed online learning for students, the state requires testing to occur in school facilities. This includes the Florida Standards Assessments, Next Generation Sunshine State Standards Assessments, and end-of-course assessments. Seniors who need to meet testing requirements for graduation must come to the campus for the SAT, ACT, PSAT Math, PERT, Algebra 1, or the Geometry end-of-course assessment to meet these requirements. To be ready for the upcoming state exams, the district wants to test its own procedures. There will be one more round of progress-monitoring exams before the higher-stakes state tests, and the district wants to use these exams as a practice run for the more important state tests later this year. Parents are asked to bring their home-based learners to the campus between Feb. 22 and March 5 for this last round of progress-monitoring exams. Following are questions and answers about in-person testing during this pandemic year. What are progress-monitoring tests? The district uses progress-monitoring assessments to see how students are progressing along Florida’s standards.","ok":true,"op":"document"},"time":17},"model_calls":26,"document_judgments":18,"relevance_3":3,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
